 "backlog": [
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "architecture",
   "id": "b033",
   "spec": "In the kernel member, replace the ECFP Tanimoto kernel with the mean of Tanimoto kernels on ECFP bits (radius 2) and on count-based Morgan features using the min/max (generalised Tanimoto) kernel. Compute count features with GetCountFingerprintAsNumPy at radius 3, 1024 bits, in featurize, stored as an extra array passed through. The kernel is sum_i min(a_i,b_i) / sum_i max(a_i,b_i), computed in chunks to bound memory. Keep the RBF term and the grid search.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Kernel ridge with mixed multi-radius Tanimoto"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "batch",
   "id": "b039",
   "spec": "Compute a weight for each training molecule from how common its Murcko scaffold is within the training fold: w_i = 1 / sqrt(count of molecules sharing its scaffold). Normalise the weights to mean 1. Pass them as sample_weight to the random forest fit and as the weight argument to lgb.Dataset in base_predict. Leave the kernel members unweighted, because the Tanimoto solve has no natural weight hook. The aim is to reduce the influence of large scaffold families on the trees.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Scaffold-frequency sample weighting"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "schedule",
   "id": "b045",
   "spec": "Reduce the LightGBM learning rate from 0.03 to 0.01 and raise num_boost_round from N_ROUNDS=600 to 1500, which usually gives a smoother L1 fit with the same capacity. Change the constant N_ROUNDS to 1500 and the learning_rate in lgb_params to 0.01. Keep num_leaves=31, min_child_samples=10, feature_fraction=0.2. Check that the job stays under the 10-minute limit; if it does not, use 1000 rounds at learning_rate 0.015 instead.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Lower learning rate with more rounds for LightGBM"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "optimizer",
   "id": "b051",
   "spec": "The kernel-ridge member currently grid-searches lambda in (0.1, 0.5, 2.0) and RBF weight in (0.5, 1.0, 2.0) using one np.linalg.solve per combination per inner fold. Restructure this so that for each RBF weight and each inner GroupKFold(n_splits=4) split, the combined kernel K = Ta[itr,itr] + wr * Ra[itr,itr] is factorised once with np.linalg.eigh giving eigenvalues s and vectors U, and then every lambda is evaluated in closed form as w_in = U @ ((U.T @ (y_tr - mu)) / (s + lam)). Because one eigendecomposition serves all lambdas, widen the grid to lam in (0.03, 0.1, 0.3, 1.0, 3.0) at roughly the current runtime. Select (lam, wr) by summed absolute validation error as now, then refit on the full training block with np.linalg.solve as before. No other part of the script changes.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Eigendecomposition sweep over a wider kernel-ridge lambda grid"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "optimizer",
   "id": "b057",
   "spec": "Replace the grid search over lam and wr in base_predict with selection by log marginal likelihood. For each (lam, wr) in a 5x5 grid, take the eigendecomposition of Ta + wr*Ra once per wr. Compute the log marginal likelihood cheaply from the eigenvalues, with the targets centred. Choose the best pair and solve once. This removes the inner CV loops and so is faster. Optionally use the saved time to add more wr values.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Gaussian-process style kernel member with learned noise via marginal likelihood"
  }
 ],
